  {
    "id": "3280146735",
    "isSimulated": false,
    "title": "Music Visualizer | iOS Style (Media Integration)",
    "description": "[img]https://i.imgur.com/KOEcClx.gif[/img]\r\n[h3]FAQ - Why can't I see the song cover?[/h3]\r\nWallpaper Engine retrieves song data from the Windows Media Overlay. If your streaming software doesn't support this feature, you won't be able to see the song information, including the cover. Additionally, make sure you have enabled the \"Media Integration Support\" setting in the \"General > Windows\" section.\r\n\r\n[h3]Why is the song cover low-resolution?[/h3]\r\nThe quality of the album cover depends entirely on the music streaming application you’re using. Wallpaper Engine simply displays the image provided by the app through the Windows Media Overlay.\r\nIf the app only sends a low-resolution version, there’s no way to improve it directly.\r\n\r\n[h3]Why don't the buttons work?[/h3]\r\n[strike]Unfortunately, this is a limitation of Wallpaper Engine itself. However, if you pause your song, you'll notice that the play/pause button will change according to the playback status.[/strike]\r\n\r\nWith version 2.7, Wallpaper Engine introduced the ability to link clickable wallpaper elements to user-defined shortcuts. This means you can now assign specific actions to buttons, including executing CMD commands on click.\r\n\r\nThis wallpaper has been updated to support User Shortcuts, so you can configure the buttons to work as intended.\r\n\r\nFor advanced users, I’ve also created a tool that makes this setup even easier: [url=https://github.com/TheGhostAle/WallpaperEngineMediaTools]Wallpaper Engine Media Tools on GitHub[/url]\r\n\r\n\r\nTags: iOS, Apple, home screen, minimalistic, audio responsive, sleek, modern, iPhone aesthetic, dynamic, real-time, animated wallpaper, vibrant, customizable, audio responsive, ultrawide, uwide",
